<BaseLayout
  title="Protein Calculator — Daily Protein Intake Range — Nawtch"
  description="Free protein calculator. Get a gram/day protein range for sedentary, active, cutting, or older-adult goals, based on cited nutrition research."
  canonicalPath="/calculators/protein/"
  bodyClass="calculators"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Protein Calculator</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Get a daily protein gram range based on your body weight and goal.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/calculators/">← Back to Calculators</a>
  </header>

  <div id="calc-protein"></div>

  <div id="calc-protein-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How Much Protein Do You Actually Need?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The U.S. Institute of Medicine's Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for protein is <strong>0.8 grams
        per kilogram of body weight per day</strong> for sedentary adults — this is the minimum intake needed to
        avoid a gradual loss of body protein, not necessarily the amount that supports optimal body composition
        or muscle health. A randomized controlled trial that overfed healthy, sedentary adults diets containing
        0.7, 1.8, or 3.0 g/kg/day of protein found that the low-protein group actually lost a small amount of
        lean body mass despite gaining weight, while both higher-protein groups gained lean mass — suggesting
        the RDA floor is lower than what benefits body composition even for people who aren't training.
        <br /><br />
        For people doing regular resistance training with a goal of building muscle, a systematic review and
        meta-analysis pooling roughly 49 studies on protein supplementation and resistance-training outcomes
        found that gains in muscle mass and strength plateaued around <strong>1.6 g/kg/day</strong>, with the
        analysis's upper confidence interval extending to about 2.2 g/kg/day — the range this calculator uses
        for the Active/Muscle Building tier.
        <br /><br />
        The same 1.6–2.2 g/kg/day range is commonly recommended during a calorie deficit as well, to protect
        lean mass while losing fat. A randomized trial in exercising adults during a marked energy deficit found
        that a group eating 2.4 g/kg/day of protein gained lean mass and lost more fat than a group eating 1.2
        g/kg/day, supporting the idea that protein needs — if anything — trend higher, not lower, while cutting.
        <br /><br />
        Older adults face a different problem: <strong>anabolic resistance</strong>, a reduced ability to build
        muscle protein from a given amount of dietary protein, which raises the minimum intake needed to
        maintain muscle mass and function. The PROT-AGE Study Group's position paper recommends adults over 65
        average at least <strong>1.0–1.2 g/kg/day</strong>, with higher intakes — up to roughly 1.5 g/kg/day or
        more — advised for those who are physically active or managing an acute or chronic illness.
      </p>
      <strong>Sources:</strong>
      <br /><br />
      Institute of Medicine (2005). <em>Dietary Reference Intakes for Energy, Carbohydrate, Fiber, Fat, Fatty
      Acids, Cholesterol, Protein, and Amino Acids.</em> National Academies Press.
      <br />
      <a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10490/chapter/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the report →</a>
      <br /><br />
      Bray, G.A., et al. (2012). <em>Effect of Dietary Protein Content on Weight Gain, Energy Expenditure, and
